# Task brief and proposed work split

The sole supplied material is Research objective, experiment-repo issue #1 (ht-admin); no external papers or data accompany it. The complete substantive objective is quoted below.

> Photon burst in simulated arrival times: a small study whose large data file must pass between positions as a release.
>
> Data. Generate exactly 30,000,000 photon arrival times in seconds with `numpy.random.default_rng(205)`: 24,000,000 background photons uniform on [0, 1200) and 6,000,000 burst photons from a normal distribution with mean 600 and standard deviation 30; concatenate, sort, and write them as raw little-endian float32 with no header, `photons.f32` (120,000,000 bytes). Do not commit this file to any repository.
>
> Release. The position that generates the data publishes it as a release of its own repository, with these files:
> - `photons.f32`, and `photons.sha256` holding its SHA-256;
> - `preview.png`: the histogram of arrival times in 1 s bins;
> - `burst.mp4`: an H.264 MP4 of a few seconds showing the histogram filling up as time advances;
> - `explorer.html`: one self-contained HTML page (no external requests) that draws the light curve and lets a reader change the bin width.
>
> Analysis. The figures and the fit are computed from `photons.f32` as downloaded from that release, by a position other than the one that generated it. Fit the light curve with a constant background plus a Gaussian burst, by Poisson maximum likelihood on 1 s bins, and give the background rate, the burst's total photon count, centre and width with 95% intervals.
>
> Report. `report/report.md`, one page: the release tag, the SHA-256 of the file as downloaded and whether it matches `photons.sha256`, the fit table, and one figure of the light curve with the fit.

## Interpretation and analysis standard

The generator must differ from both the fitting and figure-producing positions. Proposed allocation: paper-repo generates and publishes data and checksum first; experiment-repo and figtab-repo independently download that release. Experiment fits and verifies bytes/checksum; figtab makes release media and final light-curve figure from its downloaded data, and paper adds the media to its original release. This staged upload satisfies the direction of provenance without circular input dependencies.

Use 1200 one-second bins spanning [0,1200]. Record any out-of-range observations and actual extrema; do not silently discard unexpected data. Model expected bin counts as background rate times bin duration plus total Gaussian count times the normal CDF difference across bin edges. Optimize positive rate, count and width with a Poisson likelihood. Report parameter units and 95% likelihood-profile intervals, with a curvature cross-check if feasible. Check convergence, residuals and goodness-of-fit; compare fitted parameters against the generating values (20000 photons/s, 6000000 photons, 600 s, 30 s). Fixed component counts make Poisson intervals model-based, not exact repeated-sampling coverage under the fixed-total simulator; explain this distinction to the report owner. Quantization to float32 is part of the required input, and all fits and plots must use the downloaded bytes.

Required presentation: visible constant baseline and localized burst; overlay the integrated-bin fit on the light curve, with axes in seconds and photons/s (equal to counts per 1 s bin). Preview is the empirical 1 s histogram. Animation must show cumulative revealed time bins, not imply repeated simulated realizations. Explorer rebinning must be self-contained; retain sufficiently fine histogram data for supported bin widths and state supported widths.

